Route the agent host around fork() on iOS -- in-process, via worker_threads, not a second OS-launched process.

iOS denies fork()/posix_spawn() to third-party processes outright, at the
sandbox-profile level -- unrelated to JIT or code signing, the same wall
ios-exthost-no-fork.diff already routes the extension host around. This is
a separate bug: vscode's built-in AI chat/agent host (the "Build with
Agent" panel) is a genuinely different subsystem from the extension host,
launched by NodeAgentHostStarter via ipc.cp.ts's Client, which does an
unconditional real child_process.fork() and talks process.send/'message' --
a protocol worker_threads.Worker cannot speak at all (no process.send
equivalent, only parentPort.postMessage), unlike the extension host's own
protocol (a plain OS socket, agnostic to how the two ends were launched).
Confirmed as a real, reproducing crash via a captured node-stdio.log
("ServerAgentHostManager: agent host failed to start Error: spawn EPERM",
repeated until the restart budget is spent), not guessed.

Adds WorkerClient/WorkerServer to ipc.cp.ts (pure additions -- the file
watcher and pty host, which also import Client/Server from this file and
are already broken here for unrelated reasons, are unaffected), and wires
them into both ends -- NodeAgentHostStarter (main-thread side) and
agentHostMain.ts (worker-thread side) -- gated the same way the extension
host already is: IPADVSCODE_NO_FORK on the launch side, !isMainThread on
the launched side.

Not yet behaviorally verified end to end on device: no CI test exercises
the agent host at all, so this is verified only by a real tsc compile
(vscode-reh-web-build.yml's own gulp task does one over every patch in the
series), not by observed behavior. See CLAUDE.md/README before assuming
this actually works, not just compiles.

